he quarry, and the distance of it from the place where the fence is erected. The lime costs about 6d. per boll of about 4082667 bolls; and from 15 to 16 bolls of lime are used to the rood, of 36 square ells Scots measure; and there are upwards of 43 Scott ells, or 44 English yards. When the common round or flint stones are made use of, as they require more lime, it is necessary to use 30 or 35 bolls of lime to the rood. The thorns are sold from five to ten fillings per thousand, according to their age, reckoning ix feces to the hundred. Making the ditch, laying the thorns, and preparing the top of the wall, generally cost from 7d. to 8d. every six ells. About 50 carts of stones, each cart carrying from 7 to 9 cwt. will build a rood; the carriage at 2d. per cart for half a mile's distance."
